In the right-angled triangle, write $\sin\theta$, $\cos\theta$ and $\sin(90^{\circ}-\theta)$ as side ratios. Hence explain why $\sin(90^{\circ}-\theta)=\cos\theta$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,3);
\coordinate (B) at (0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw (0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0);
\path (A)--(B) node[midway,left] {$q$};
\path (B)--(C) node[midway,below] {$p$};
\path (A)--(C) node[midway,above right] {$r$};
\draw (0,2.5) arc[start angle=270,end angle=323.13,radius=0.5];
\path (0.35,2.15) node {$\theta$};
\draw (3.3,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=143.13,radius=0.7];
\path (2.55,0.4) node {$90^{\circ}-\theta$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
